inear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "91717b101ec401919fdb69212d18af096ca13dd287dca5f5004fc2e92603b925" "basis" 1576 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-42041" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}1+2z \\\\ -3z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "91717b101ec401919fdb69212d18af096ca13dd287dca5f5004fc2e92603b925" "basis" 1576 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-42019" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x-2y+2z=2 \\\\  & y-z=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "91717b101ec401919fdb69212d18af096ca13dd287dca5f5004fc2e92603b925" "basis" 1576 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-42019" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x=0 \\\\  & y-z=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=z-1 \\\\ z=z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $z$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ z-1 \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "91717b101ec401919fdb69212d18af096ca13dd287dca5f5004fc2e92603b925" "basis" 1576 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-42019" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bbf8c84288812bc42555f6b12911137b4fe0e0cf7c142f3b33edf7624d0a64c3" "basis" 1576 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-42019" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "91717b101ec401919fdb69212d18af096ca13dd287dca5f5004fc2e92603b925" "basis" 1576 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-42019" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "91717b101ec401919fdb69212d18af096ca13dd287dca5f5004fc2e92603b925" "basis" 1576 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-42019" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}-z \\\\ z-1 \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "91717b101ec401919fdb69212d18af096ca13dd287dca5f5004fc2e92603b925" "basis" 1576 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-42019" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ z-1 \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "91717b101ec401919fdb69212d18af096ca13dd287dca5f5004fc2e92603b925" "basis" 1576 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-42019" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}1-z \\\\ 0 \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "91717b101ec401919fdb69212d18af096ca13dd287dca5f5004fc2e92603b925" "basis" 1576 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-42019" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}2-z \\\\ 0 \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "91717b101ec401919fdb69212d18af096ca13dd287dca5f5004fc2e92603b925" "basis" 1576 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-131719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x+4y=-1 \\\\  & 2x+8y=-2 \\\\  & -3x-12y=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The system is inconsistent\n- The system is consistent dependent\n- The system has a unique solution" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "91717b101ec401919fdb69212d18af096ca13dd287dca5f5004fc2e92603b925" "basis" 1576 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-131719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To classify the system, we first need to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 8 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -12 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 8 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -12 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -12 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x,$ and we have one free variable, $y.$ That means we can find $x$ in terms of $y.$ \n\nTherefore, since there are infinitely many solutions, we conclude that the system is *consistent dependent*. \n\nAs a result, only statement II is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "91717b101ec401919fdb69212d18af096ca13dd287dca5f5004fc2e92603b925" "basis" 1576 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-131719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bbf8c84288812bc42555f6b12911137b4fe0e0cf7c142f3b33edf7624d0a64c3" "basis" 1576 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-131719" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "91717b101ec401919fdb69212d18af096ca13dd287dca5f5004fc2e92603b925" "basis" 1576 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-131719" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "91717b101ec401919fdb69212d18af096ca13dd287dca5f5004fc2e92603b925" "basis" 1576 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-131719" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "91717b101ec401919fdb69212d18af096ca13dd287dca5f5004fc2e92603b925" "basis" 1576 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-131719" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "91717b101ec401919fdb69212d18af096ca13dd287dca5f5004fc2e92603b925" "basis" 1576 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-131719" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "91717b101ec401919fdb69212d18af096ca13dd287dca5f5004fc2e92603b925" "basis" 1576 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-131719" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "None"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "91717b101ec401919fdb69212d18af096ca13dd287dca5f5004fc2e92603b925" "basis" 1576 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-131719" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "91717b101ec401919fdb69212d18af096ca13dd287dca5f5004fc2e92603b925" "basis" 1576 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-32246" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is consistent dependent:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & 3x+4y=12 \\\\  & x+ky=4\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "91717b101ec401919fdb69212d18af096ca13dd287dca5f5004fc2e92603b925" "basis" 1576 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-32246" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 & 12 \\\\ 1 & k & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 & 12 \\\\ 1 & k & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & k & 4 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & k & 4 \\\\ 0 & 4-3k & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x+ky=4 \\\\  & (4-3k)y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is consistent dependent if it has infinitely many solutions. For the system to have infinitely many solutions, it must have at least one free variable.\n\nIf $k=\\frac{4}{3},$ then the second equation is $0=0,$ which is true for any value of $y$, and we can remove this equation from the system. As a result, the system will contain only one equation, $x+ky=4.$ \n\nThen, the basic variable will be $x,$ and we will have a free variable $y.$ That means the system will have infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, our system is consistent dependent when $k=\\frac{4}{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "91717b101ec401919fdb69212d18af096ca13dd287dca5f5004fc2e92603b925" "basis" 1576 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-32246" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bbf8c84288812bc42555f6b12911137b4fe0e0cf7c142f3b33edf7624d0a64c3" "basis" 1576 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-32246" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "91717b101ec401919fdb69212d18af096ca13dd287dca5f5004fc2e92603b925" "basis" 1576 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-4711" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the system of linear equations\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/assets/a50d551e5d8abe8d9ae48778b195c382bbbf96f6fc5083c41b67642e99deffda.png)\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/example-4711.json" "file_sha256" "e2adb0de8abfc6de2c8095a2b427d0653155ac6ceb41323ad57fc50d10a2b847" "basis" 1576 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-4711" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The augmented matrix for this system is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/assets/48c423256fbdc0d7e4f6ff716fb449efe6f98bdf57ba90353bcced930ea4ed17.png)\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/assets/81b7626ac30b06c5eeb10be274dd93476a86dce5fe3053722d96e16b78d7f165.png)\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $z,$ and we have one free variable, $y.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $z$ in terms of $y.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-1-5y \\\\ y=y \\\\ z=3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1-5y \\\\ y \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/example-4711.json" "file_sha256" "e2adb0de8abfc6de2c8095a2b427d0653155ac6ceb41323ad57fc50d10a2b847" "basis" 1576 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-6173" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the system\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/assets/5f1aaf29a3dd8b7ea2c4f7104d532f7b56f32a23ccebbcfa0d1bfe86c772cc5f.png)\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The system is consistent dependent\n\n- The system has no solution\n\n- The system is consistent independent" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/example-6173.json" "file_sha256" "069b62aacb001f4e3286f0b3ffd7c22afdf4f8de90f5ea3fbb6c4c79135e894b" "basis" 1576 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-6173" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To classify the system, we first need to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/assets/6171d242b0a3887802f629de79cb952d47711a581e88db2d9304a5db356dfb62.png)\n\nUsing Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 6 & -8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/assets/80aff0fd62e3e1aeed17017c864d8d0b875b556ce1a29a89e6c61e632bf9c733.png)\n\nSince any numbers $x$ and $y$ form a solution of the final equation $0x+0y=0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of just a single equation:\n\n$$\n2x -3y= 4\n$$\n\nThe basic variable is $x,$ and we have a free variable $y.$ That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y.$ \n\nTherefore, since there are infinitely many solutions, we conclude that the system is *consistent dependent*.\n\nAs a result, only statement I is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/example-6173.json" "file_sha256" "069b62aacb001f4e3286f0b3ffd7c22afdf4f8de90f5ea3fbb6c4c79135e894b" "basis" 1576 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-4712" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is inconsistent:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x-2y=1, \\\\ 4x+ky=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/example-4712.json" "file_sha256" "31a00ffb86ea7799eb53128b8d45d4ee583fa2d9f3b626e49737b32b6836892a" "basis" 1576 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-4712" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/assets/a115d3da691619918bf92d914acdfdd95088e9a141a82ca3fe3230fa7fbb8172.png)\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & k & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & k+4 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/assets/cfea36ae2b5cb5d8715480c1e0ee0b5ba06cf39d0c2032eb6e9b9078bf8c5bde.png)\n\nThe system is inconsistent if it has no solutions. For the system to have no solutions, one of the equations above must be false.\n\nIf $k=-4,$ then the second equation is $0=-5,$ which is false, and the system will have no solution.\n\nTherefore, our system is inconsistent when $k=-4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/example-4712.json" "file_sha256" "31a00ffb86ea7799eb53128b8d45d4ee583fa2d9f3b626e49737b32b6836892a" "basis" 1576 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-131715" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=2 \\\\ {x}_{1}-3{x}_{3}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-131715" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe corresponding system is now  \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & {x}_{1}-3{x}_{3}=1 \\\\  & {x}_{2}-5{x}_{3}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ and we have one free variable, ${x}_{3}.$ That means we need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ in terms of ${x}_{3}.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=3{x}_{3}+1 \\\\ {x}_{2}=5{x}_{3}-1 \\\\ {x}_{3}={x}_{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{3}$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{3}+1 \\\\ 5{x}_{3}-1 \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-131715" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "eb6403ef7dadf6d25150ee641ffaf91c42ae50865f2b2f311f6e3123c30af872"} {"question" "q-131715" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-131715" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1-3{x}_{3} \\\\ 1-5{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{3}+1 \\\\ 1-5{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{3}+1 \\\\ 5{x}_{3}-1 \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{3}-1 \\\\ 5{x}_{3}+1 \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["text" "No solution"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-131715" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{3}+1 \\\\ 5{x}_{3}-1 \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-32247" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=4 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-32247" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -3 & -2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -3 & -2 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 10 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe corresponding system is now  \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & {x}_{1}+10{x}_{3}=-8 \\\\  & {x}_{2}-6{x}_{3}=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ and we have one free variable, ${x}_{3}.$ That means we need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ in terms of ${x}_{3}.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=-8-10{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2}=6+6{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}={x}_{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{3}$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-8-10{x}_{3} \\\\ 6+6{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-32247" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "eb6403ef7dadf6d25150ee641ffaf91c42ae50865f2b2f311f6e3123c30af872"} {"question" "q-32247" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-32247" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-8-10{x}_{3} \\\\ 6+6{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-8-6{x}_{3} \\\\ 10+6{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-8-8{x}_{3} \\\\ 6-6{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-8-{x}_{3} \\\\ 6+{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}8-{x}_{3} \\\\ 3+3{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-32247" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}-8-10{x}_{3} \\\\ 6+6{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-42041" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x-4z=2 \\\\ 4x+y-5z=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-42041" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -5 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -5 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -5 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-2z=1 \\\\ y+3z=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=1+2z \\\\ y=-3z \\\\ z=z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $z$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1+2z \\\\ -3z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-42041" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "eb6403ef7dadf6d25150ee641ffaf91c42ae50865f2b2f311f6e3123c30af872"} {"question" "q-42041" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-42041" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-2z \\\\ 4z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1+2z \\\\ -3z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1+4z \\\\ 3z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2+4z \\\\ -3z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2+4z \\\\ 1-3z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-42041" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}1+2z \\\\ -3z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-42019" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x-2y+2z=2 \\\\  & y-z=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-42019" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x=0 \\\\  & y-z=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=z-1 \\\\ z=z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $z$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ z-1 \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-42019" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "eb6403ef7dadf6d25150ee641ffaf91c42ae50865f2b2f311f6e3123c30af872"} {"question" "q-42019" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-42019" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-z \\\\ z-1 \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ z-1 \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1-z \\\\ 0 \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2-z \\\\ 0 \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-42019" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ z-1 \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-32517" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & 2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}=-4 \\\\  & {x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-32517" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now   \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & {x}_{1}=4 \\\\  & {x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ and we have one free variable, ${x}_{3}.$ Since we have that ${x}_{1}=4$,  we only need to find ${x}_{2}$ in terms of ${x}_{3}.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=4 \\\\ {x}_{2}=-6-2{x}_{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{3}$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -6-2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-32517" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "eb6403ef7dadf6d25150ee641ffaf91c42ae50865f2b2f311f6e3123c30af872"} {"question" "q-32517" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-32517" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 6-2{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -6-2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 6+{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -4-2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -6+{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-32517" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -6-2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-32242" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & -x+2y=6 \\\\  & 2x-3y=13 \\\\  & 5x-y=45\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The system is consistent dependent\n- The system is inconsistent\n- The system has a unique solution" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-32242" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To classify the system, we first need to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 13 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 45\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 13 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 45\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 25 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 45\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 25 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 75\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-9){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 25 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -150\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final row corresponds to the equation $0x+0y=-150,$ and this has no solution. Therefore, our system has no solution, and we conclude that the system is inconsistent.\n\nAs a result, only statement II is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-32242" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "eb6403ef7dadf6d25150ee641ffaf91c42ae50865f2b2f311f6e3123c30af872"} {"question" "q-32242" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-32242" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-32242" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-131719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x+4y=-1 \\\\  & 2x+8y=-2 \\\\  & -3x-12y=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The system is inconsistent\n- The system is consistent dependent\n- The system has a unique solution" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-131719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To classify the system, we first need to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 8 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -12 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 8 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -12 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -12 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x,$ and we have one free variable, $y.$ That means we can find $x$ in terms of $y.$ \n\nTherefore, since there are infinitely many solutions, we conclude that the system is *consistent dependent*. \n\nAs a result, only statement II is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-131719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "eb6403ef7dadf6d25150ee641ffaf91c42ae50865f2b2f311f6e3123c30af872"} {"question" "q-131719" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-131719" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-131719" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-131723" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x-y+z=3 \\\\  & -3x+3y-3z=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The system is inconsistent\n- The system has a unique solution\n- The system is consistent dependent" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-131723" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To classify the system, we first need to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -3 & 10\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -3 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 19\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final row corresponds to the equation $0x+0y+0z=19,$ and this has no solution. Therefore, our system has no solution, and we conclude that the system is inconsistent.\n\nAs a result, only statement I is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-131723" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "eb6403ef7dadf6d25150ee641ffaf91c42ae50865f2b2f311f6e3123c30af872"} {"question" "q-131723" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-131723" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-131723" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-74634" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & 2x-y=-1 \\\\  & 2x-4y=3 \\\\  & x+y=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The system is consistent dependent\n- The system has a unique solution\n- The system is inconsistent" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-74634" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To classify the system, we will first try to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is \n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final row corresponds to the equation $0x+0y=\\frac{9}{2},$ and it has no solution. Therefore, our system has no solution, and we conclude that the system is inconsistent.\n\nAs a result, only statement III is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-74634" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "eb6403ef7dadf6d25150ee641ffaf91c42ae50865f2b2f311f6e3123c30af872"} {"question" "q-74634" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-74634" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-74634" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-74635" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & -2x+4y+3z=0 \\\\  & x+3y=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The system has no solution\n- The system is consistent independent\n- The system is consistent dependent" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-74635" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To classify the system, we first try to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & \\frac{3}{2} & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means that  we can find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$ \n\nTherefore, since there are infinitely many solutions, we conclude that the system is *consistent dependent*. \n\nAs a result, only statement III is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-74635" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "eb6403ef7dadf6d25150ee641ffaf91c42ae50865f2b2f311f6e3123c30af872"} {"question" "q-74635" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-74635" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-74635" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-131728" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is inconsistent:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & -x+4y=-2 \\\\  & -3x+(3k+15)y=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-131728" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 3k+15 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 3k+15 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3k+3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-x+4y=-2 \\\\ (3k+3)y=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is inconsistent if it has no solutions. For the system to have no solutions, one of the equations above must be false.\n\nIf $k=-1,$ then the second equation is $0=2,$ which is false, and the system will have no solution.\n\nTherefore, our system is inconsistent when $k=-1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-131728" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "eb6403ef7dadf6d25150ee641ffaf91c42ae50865f2b2f311f6e3123c30af872"} {"question" "q-131728" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-131728" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "k=-1"] ["math" "k=-2"] ["math" "k=0"] ["math" "k=1"] ["math" "k=2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-131728" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "k=-1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-32246" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is consistent dependent:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & 3x+4y=12 \\\\  & x+ky=4\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-32246" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 & 12 \\\\ 1 & k & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 & 12 \\\\ 1 & k & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & k & 4 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & k & 4 \\\\ 0 & 4-3k & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x+ky=4 \\\\  & (4-3k)y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is consistent dependent if it has infinitely many solutions. For the system to have infinitely many solutions, it must have at least one free variable.\n\nIf $k=\\frac{4}{3},$ then the second equation is $0=0,$ which is true for any value of $y$, and we can remove this equation from the system. As a result, the system will contain only one equation, $x+ky=4.$ \n\nThen, the basic variable will be $x,$ and we will have a free variable $y.$ That means the system will have infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, our system is consistent dependent when $k=\\frac{4}{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-32246" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "eb6403ef7dadf6d25150ee641ffaf91c42ae50865f2b2f311f6e3123c30af872"} {"question" "q-32246" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-32246" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "k=-4"] ["math" "k=-\\frac{4}{3}"] ["math" "k=0"] ["math" "k=\\frac{3}{4}"] ["math" "k=\\frac{4}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-32246" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "k=\\frac{4}{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-74688" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is inconsistent:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x+2y=-4 \\\\  & 3x+2ky=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-74688" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The augmented matrix for this system is \n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 2k & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 2k & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 2k-6 & 10\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+2y=-4 \\\\ (2k-6)y=10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is inconsistent if it has no solutions. For the system to have no solutions, one of the equations above must be false.\n\nIf $k=3,$ then the second equation is $0=10,$ which is false, and the system will have no solution.\n\nTherefore, our system is inconsistent when $k=3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-74688" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "eb6403ef7dadf6d25150ee641ffaf91c42ae50865f2b2f311f6e3123c30af872"} {"question" "q-74688" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-74688" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "k=-1"] ["math" "k=-2"] ["math" "k=-3"] ["math" "k=2"] ["math" "k=3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-74688" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "k=3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-32245" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is inconsistent:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & 2x-y=3 \\\\  & 4x+ky=4\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-32245" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & k & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & k & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & k+2 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & 2x-y=3 \\\\  & (k+2)y=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is inconsistent if it has no solutions. For the system to have no solutions, one of the equations above must be false.\n\nNow if $k=-2$, then the second equation is $0=-2,$ which is false, and the system will have no solution.\n\nTherefore, our system is inconsistent when  $k=-2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-32245" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "eb6403ef7dadf6d25150ee641ffaf91c42ae50865f2b2f311f6e3123c30af872"} {"question" "q-32245" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-32245" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "k=-2"] ["math" "k=-4"] ["math" "k=0"] ["math" "k=2"] ["math" "k=4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-32245" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "k=-2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-32331" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is inconsistent:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-4x-y=2 \\\\ 8x+ky=5\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-32331" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 8 & k & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 8 & k & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & k-2 & 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-4x-y=2 \\\\ (k-2)y=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is inconsistent if it has no solutions. For the system to have no solutions, one of the equations above must be false.\n\nIf $k=2,$ then the second equation is $0=9,$ which is false, and the system will have no solution.\n\nTherefore, our system is inconsistent when $k=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-32331" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "eb6403ef7dadf6d25150ee641ffaf91c42ae50865f2b2f311f6e3123c30af872"} {"question" "q-32331" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-32331" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "k=-2"] ["math" "k=-5"] ["math" "k=-8"] ["math" "k=0"] ["math" "k=2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "q-32331" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "k=2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed3992e92b167a1726e9827f61f579a65993fc8203a08a7418d18a0c736a816e"} {"question" "e-4711" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the system of linear equations\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/assets/a50d551e5d8abe8d9ae48778b195c382bbbf96f6fc5083c41b67642e99deffda.png)\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/example-4711.json" "file_sha256" "e4cb7a8b8f6a6019f0d0d8b83ee989a4b50d83523f1c82947bee1fdbc9b40423"} {"question" "e-4711" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The augmented matrix for this system is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/assets/48c423256fbdc0d7e4f6ff716fb449efe6f98bdf57ba90353bcced930ea4ed17.png)\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/assets/81b7626ac30b06c5eeb10be274dd93476a86dce5fe3053722d96e16b78d7f165.png)\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $z,$ and we have one free variable, $y.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $z$ in terms of $y.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-1-5y \\\\ y=y \\\\ z=3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1-5y \\\\ y \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/example-4711.json" "file_sha256" "e4cb7a8b8f6a6019f0d0d8b83ee989a4b50d83523f1c82947bee1fdbc9b40423"} {"question" "e-6173" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the system\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/assets/427c19efbebba6bfe2dc36bccd852d7a1f5e0ec97dc46c00043d019807e86ec2.png)\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The system is consistent dependent\n\n- The system has no solution\n\n- The system is consistent independent" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/example-6173.json" "file_sha256" "b37a91bc731542690d484c0276ae00cf0ab379518c501370b09ce3887d56864e"} {"question" "e-6173" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To classify the system, we first need to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/assets/50f13deaa03c2af30f511ef3d514e3dd2e118d81d9b121b436ff1a2f2497c303.png)\n\nUsing Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 6 & -8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/assets/e02bb5004f792eea20216e64098bb924dbed15defb9330967d6ae5a144e067dc.png)\n\nSince any numbers $x$ and $y$ form a solution of the final equation $0x+0y=0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of just a single equation:\n\n$$\n2x -3y= 4\n$$\n\nThe basic variable is $x,$ and we have a free variable $y.$ That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y.$ \n\nTherefore, since there are infinitely many solutions, we conclude that the system is *consistent dependent*.\n\nAs a result, only statement I is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/example-6173.json" "file_sha256" "b37a91bc731542690d484c0276ae00cf0ab379518c501370b09ce3887d56864e"} {"question" "e-4712" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is inconsistent:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x-2y=1, \\\\ 4x+ky=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/example-4712.json" "file_sha256" "8cc7578faeada27269e20c95b18060672cec5c8578e78439af36c2e3162386d7"} {"question" "e-4712" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/assets/f4478e5828da7c79a47bf3ca212ed15357befe7b0b208c78beb0895b445272c9.png)\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & k & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & k+4 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/assets/cfea36ae2b5cb5d8715480c1e0ee0b5ba06cf39d0c2032eb6e9b9078bf8c5bde.png)\n\nThe system is inconsistent if it has no solutions. For the system to have no solutions, one of the equations above must be false.\n\nIf $k=-4,$ then the second equation is $0=-5,$ which is false, and the system will have no solution.\n\nTherefore, our system is inconsistent when $k=-4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079703/example-4712.json" "file_sha256" "8cc7578faeada27269e20c95b18060672cec5c8578e78439af36c2e3162386d7"}] "usage" {"q-131719" [] "q-32242" [] "e-4712" [] "q-42019" [] "q-32246" [] "e-4711" [] "e-6173" [] "q-42041" []} "basis" 2826 "retractions" [[17592186115671 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{rl} & - x + 2 y = 6 \\\\  & 2 x - 3 y = 13 \\\\  & 5 x - y = 45\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The system is consistent dependent\n2. The system is inconsistent\n3. The system has a unique solution"] [17592186114987 :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/assets/a50d551e5d8abe8d9ae48778b195c382bbbf96f6fc5083c41b67642e99deffda.png)\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system."] [17592186114987 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/assets/48c423256fbdc0d7e4f6ff716fb449efe6f98bdf57ba90353bcced930ea4ed17.png)\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/assets/81b7626ac30b06c5eeb10be274dd93476a86dce5fe3053722d96e16b78d7f165.png)\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $z,$ and we have one free variable, $y.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $z$ in terms of $y.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-1-5y \\\\ y=y \\\\ z=3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1-5y \\\\ y \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186115041 :question/problem "Given the system\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/assets/5f1aaf29a3dd8b7ea2c4f7104d532f7b56f32a23ccebbcfa0d1bfe86c772cc5f.png)\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The system is consistent dependent\n\n- The system has no solution\n\n- The system is consistent independent"] [17592186115041 :question/worked-solution "To classify the system, we first need to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/assets/6171d242b0a3887802f629de79cb952d47711a581e88db2d9304a5db356dfb62.png)\n\nUsing Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 6 & -8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/assets/80aff0fd62e3e1aeed17017c864d8d0b875b556ce1a29a89e6c61e632bf9c733.png)\n\nSince any numbers $x$ and $y$ form a solution of the final equation $0x+0y=0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of just a single equation:\n\n$$\n2x -3y= 4\n$$\n\nThe basic variable is $x,$ and we have a free variable $y.$ That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y.$ \n\nTherefore, since there are infinitely many solutions, we conclude that the system is *consistent dependent*.\n\nAs a result, only statement I is true."] [17592186114988 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/assets/a115d3da691619918bf92d914acdfdd95088e9a141a82ca3fe3230fa7fbb8172.png)\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & k & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & k+4 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/assets/cfea36ae2b5cb5d8715480c1e0ee0b5ba06cf39d0c2032eb6e9b9078bf8c5bde.png)\n\nThe system is inconsistent if it has no solutions. For the system to have no solutions, one of the equations above must be false.\n\nIf $k=-4,$ then the second equation is $0=-5,$ which is false, and the system will have no solution.\n\nTherefore, our system is inconsistent when $k=-4.$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186113427 17592186113428 17592186113429 17592186113430 17592186113431 17592186113433 17592186113434 17592186113435 17592186113436 17592186113437 17592186317567 17592186317568 17592186317569 17592186317570 17592186317571 17592186317616 17592186317617 17592186317618 17592186317619 17592186317620 17592186317630 17592186317631 17592186317632 17592186317633 17592186317634] "immutable_fields" [[17592186113426 :answer-field/correct] [17592186113426 :answer-field/id] [17592186113426 :answer-field/key] [17592186113426 :answer-field/type] [17592186113432 :answer-field/correct] [17592186113432 :answer-field/id] [17592186113432 :answer-field/key] [17592186113432 :answer-field/type] [17592186317566 :answer-field/correct] [17592186317566 :answer-field/id] [17592186317566 :answer-field/key] [17592186317566 :answer-field/type] [17592186317615 :answer-field/correct] [17592186317615 :answer-field/id] [17592186317615 :answer-field/key] [17592186317615 :answer-field/type] [17592186317629 :answer-field/correct] [17592186317629 :answer-field/id] [17592186317629 :answer-field/key] [17592186317629 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
